<article class="env-lemma" id="098W">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/098W">Lemma <span data-tag="098W">61.12.20</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $S_{affine, {pro\text{-}\acute{e}tale}}$ denote the full subcategory of $S_{pro\text{-}\acute{e}tale}$ consisting of affine objects. A covering of $S_{affine, {pro\text{-}\acute{e}tale}}$ will be a standard pro-étale covering, see Definition <a href="/tag/098E" data-tag="098E">61.12.6</a>. Then restriction </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathcal{F} \longmapsto \mathcal{F}|_{S_{affine, {\acute{e}tale}}}  \]
</div>
  <p> defines an equivalence of topoi $\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (S_{pro\text{-}\acute{e}tale}) \cong \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (S_{affine, {pro\text{-}\acute{e}tale}})$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This you can show directly from the definitions, and is a good exercise. But it also follows immediately from Sites, Lemma <a href="/tag/03A0" data-tag="03A0">7.29.1</a> by checking that the inclusion functor $S_{affine, {pro\text{-}\acute{e}tale}} \to S_{pro\text{-}\acute{e}tale}$ is a special cocontinuous functor (see Sites, Definition <a href="/tag/03CG" data-tag="03CG">7.29.2</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>